It's fairly clear (to me, anyway :-) that the two most important stats in 
Nethack are Str and Int. Without decent Str you can carry only the barest
minimum, which is a pain, and more importantly it is *much* harder to
kill monsters by fighting with poor Str (given the same Dex, of course!)
Without decent Int, reading spellbooks is often a deadly process, and even
if this works actually casting those spells is a pretty dicey procedure
anyway. Dex is pretty important too, but it doesn't
differ as greatly between characters, whereas there is a vast amount of
difference between, say, a Valkyrie and a Wizard in terms of Str and Int.
My gripe is this: although it's not a great problem to increase a low Str
(gauntlets of power, giant corpses, spinach, royal jelly, etc. ad nauseam)
it is practically impossible to elevate a character with poor Int to
spellcasting ability, short of a crate of blessed potions of gain ability.
Helms of brilliance are pretty rare, especially when compared to GoP, and
even then the Int is only increased by the enchantment, not simply to 25.
Thus, if you want to cast spells in the game, your only real option is
to start off as a Wizard and build up Str as you go. 

My suggestion is this: let there be more ways to increase intelligence! 
Eating wizard or Lich corpses may be one way (I fully agree with the
idea of making tough monsters *useful*, to prevent genocide abuse). Perhaps
there could be a Spellbook of Learned Knowledge which would be easy to read
and would increase your Int, but give no spell (unless you are already smart:
	You begin to memorize the runes... --more--
	The plaid spellbook is very dull... --more--
	You fall asleep... --more--   )
Of course, you would have to identify it first, or risk the usual paralysation
and death by iguana after reading a spell-yielding spellbook.

Another way may be by wishing. I think that wishes could be improved so
that you can wish not only for objects, but also for:
  "You may make a wish. What do you want to wish for?"	
	- Stats: "3 points of intelligence" "2 points of dexterity" etc.
     Of course, you could just ask for blessed potions of gain ability.
     But you should have more chance of getting 4 points of some stat than
     the 4 potions, so a dumb character doesn't have to take the risk of only
     getting 1 point when he _needs_ lots. Wishing for "3 points of
     intelligence" is also better than, say, "blessed +3 helm of brilliance":
     it is permanent, and you may already have a helmet.
	- Intrinsics: "fire resistance" "telepathy" "speed" "water walking"
     At the moment, you could just wish for the appropriate ring or armour
     to do the job. This method does not use up any needed ring finger or
     body part, and is permanent (you can lose rings, etc.)
     Also, you should be able to wish for auto-identification, as per
     priests (holiness), barbarians (weapons) etc. This should be an 	
     intrinsic, not simply an attribute of the (human) monster. Thus
     priests automatically have "holy insight", knights have "armour knowledge"
     etc. 
	- Special rooms: "holy altar" "used bookstore" "chaotic temple"
          "graveyard" (more interesting than cursed-genocide wraiths)
     Wish for it, and there it is on the next level! No more complaining
     about 'I was on level 2742317 and _still_ no lawful altar!!'
This would make wishes a bit more interesting, and give the player more
of a choice to make, instead of the standard 3 blessed gain abilities, etc.

Comments, anyone...?


